{
 "id": "1903-puffer-balancing",
 "name": "Balancing two lines in a picture frame (Puffer 1903 symmetry)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Ethel D. Puffer's experiments on size in \"Studies in symmetry\" (Harvard Psychological Studies 1, 1903, pp. 467–539; Section III B, Experiments I (a) and (b)). On a black picture field (600 × 400 mm in the original) inside a grey frame, a white upright strip is fixed at 40, 80, 120, 160 and 200 mm from the centre; the participant slides a second strip left or right to the position that is most pleasing \"for its own sake\" (not \"does it balance?\"), or says that no position pleases. In (a) the fixed strip is short (80 × 10 mm) and the movable one long (160 × 10 mm); in (b) the reverse. Each setting is stored in millimetres from the centre with Puffer's sign convention (minus = same side as the fixed strip; X = refusal), so results compare with her tables of choice types: mechanical balance (larger strip nearer the centre), mirror symmetry, positions near the centre, and same-side placements. Differences from the original study: (1) the field is drawn on screen instead of white cardboard strips on a black cloth board lit in a dark room; (2) the fixed strip's side (left/right) is random per setting and stored, as Puffer found no consistent left/right effect; (3) one choice per setting instead of repeated sessions with optional second choices; the movable strip starts at a random position; (4) the double-line Experiment II and later experiments are left out; gender, age and art training are asked at the end. Public domain source.",
